{{Economy of Balisca}}
The '''Pirihueico Canal''' ({{Langx|bl|Canal de Pirihueico}}) or the '''Pirihueico Seaway''' is an artificial {{convert |82|km|adj=on|abbr=off}} waterway in [[Cerdanya]] that connects the [[Salsipuedes Sea]] with the southern [[Sea of Shuainia]], cutting through the [[W:Fjord|fjords]] and [[W:Ria|rias]] of the peninsula of [[Wüñülelfün]], and is a [[W:Channel (geography)|conduit]] for intra-Baliscan maritime trade.

Construction of the canal began  in 1881, but was halted in 1887 because of a lack of investors' confidence due to engineering problems and a high worker mortality rate. The outbreak of the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] in 1893 resulted in a surge in interest for an alternative to the [[Balboa Canal]], leading to the resumption of work on the project. The canal was completed and opened to transit in 1896.

The Pirihueico Canal shortcut greatly reduces the time for ships to travel between the [[Sea of Shuainia]] and the Salsipuedes Sea, allowing them to avoid the lengthy and hazardous routes around [[Pelullafken]] and through the archipelago of [[Cap de Pingüins]]. This maritime region is commonly referred to as the [[Salsipuedes Graveyard]]. 

